{
  "probability": 0.23,
  "range_low": 0.16,
  "range_high": 0.30,
  "summary": [
    "Historical base rate for a 2/3 majority in Hungary (133+ seats) is about 44% (4 of 9 elections since 1990), which provides a non-trivial anchor for a 2/3 outcome.",
    "Late-2025/early-2026 polls show the opposition around or ahead of Fidesz in several measures, reducing the probability of any single party achieving a 2/3 majority under current conditions.",
    "Seat projections cluster well below the 2/3 threshold (roughly 127–133 seats depending on model), with Real-PR/21 Research Center estimates around 64% of seats for Fidesz–KDNP.",
    "Structural factors like district-level gerrymandering can offer upside to Fidesz, potentially offsetting national polling drifts under favorable turnout.",
    "Turnout dynamics and market signals add uncertainty; overall, a 2/3 majority by any party remains unlikely but not impossible, especially if the anti-Fidesz bloc unites and turnout concentrates."
  ],
  "confidence": "medium",
  "narrative": "Starting from a 44% historical base rate for a 2/3 majority, current polling and seat-model signals tilt away from a guaranteed 133+ seat outcome for any single party, though districting and turnout dynamics keep a narrow path open. The central estimate lands around low-to-mid teens in probability, with a wide but credible range reflecting substantial uncertainty in a highly contested election."
  ,
  "justification": {
    "anchor": {
      "probability": 0.44,
      "basis": "Two-thirds majority (133+ seats) in the Hungarian National Assembly has occurred in 4 of 9 elections since 1990, providing a historical reference class and an anchor around 0.44."
    },
    "factor_updates": [
      {
        "factor": "Opposition consolidation behind Tisza",
        "direction": "down",
        "magnitude": "strong",
        "rationale": "Consolidation reduces fragmentation and concentrates anti-Fidesz votes, making a clean 2/3 win by a single bloc less likely and lowering the probability of any 2/3 outcome."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Tisza polling lead vs Fidesz",
        "direction": "down",
        "magnitude": "strong",
        "rationale": "Polls in Jan–Feb 2026 show Tisza ahead or competitive, diminishing confidence in a 2/3 majority for Fidesz and reducing overall probability of a 2/3 outcome."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Seat projections near but below the 2/3 threshold",
        "direction": "down",
        "magnitude": "moderate",
        "rationale": "Models project around 64% of seats for Fidesz-KDNP but well below 133 seats; this caps the likelihood of any 2/3 majority under current distributions."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Historical base rate for 2/3 majorities in Hungary",
        "direction": "down",
        "magnitude": "weak",
        "rationale": "Although a 2/3 majority has occurred before, it is not a default outcome; the historical rate (44%) grounds the anchor but cannot overpower current signals."
      },
      {
        "factor": "District-level design and electoral-system factors",
        "direction": "up",
        "magnitude": "moderate",
        "rationale": "Gerrymandering could lift seat shares for the largest bloc under favorable turnout, providing upside that prevents a clean downward drift from being definitive."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Our Homeland (MH) crossing threshold / vote-splitting",
        "direction": "down",
        "magnitude": "moderate",
        "rationale": "Third-party crossing and vote fragmentation can dilute any bloc’s path to 133 seats, reducing the likelihood of a 2/3 outcome for either side."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Market signals of political-change credibility",
        "direction": "up",
        "magnitu"
        "e": "weak",
        "rationale": "Financial commentators anticipate implications of a possible political shift, which can indirectly increase the perceived viability of non-2/3 outcomes."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Turnout dynamics favoring opposition mobilization",
        "direction": "down",
        "magnitude": "moderate",
        "rationale": "Higher turnout among anti-Fidesz voters can compress Fidesz seat shares, reducing the odds of a 2/3 majority."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Divergent polling landscape across firms",
        "direction": "neutral",
        "magnitude": "weak",
        "rationale": "Inconsistent poll signals introduce uncertainty and prevent a single strong inference about a 2/3 outcome."
      }
    ],
    "synthesis_rationale": "Starting from the 0.44 anchor, downward updates from opposition consolidation, Tisza polling leads, and modest seat-model evidence push probability lower. Upward pressures from districting and potential turnout effects create a partial offset, while market signals and poll diversity inject further uncertainty. The net synthesis yields a central estimate around 0.23 with a plausible range of 0.16–0.30, acknowledging that non-linear dynamics in multi-party contests can produce outsized surprises. Calibration acknowledges limited predictive power of polls in a mixed-electoral system and the possibility of abrupt shifts in turnout or realignments."
  }
